d aborts, without calling the 500 handler. Wrapped copies of the error are caught too. The Go standard library server also skips the stack trace for this error, which is why I matched that.\n- `recovery_test.go`: new test `TestPanicWithAbortHandler`. It checks that the status code the handler set is kept and that the log has the short message but no \"panic recovered\" stack dump.\n\n**Why not more:** the broken-pipe handling already does what's needed, so the fix is 5 added lines with no new code path.\n\n**Checks run**\n- Before the fix, the new test failed for the reason the change targets: it got a 500 and a full stack trace. With `recovery.go` reverted, it fails again.\n- With the fix, the panic/recovery tests pass and `go test ./...` passes in every package.\n- `gofmt` and `go vet` report nothing.\n\n**Not done:** the commit. To finish, set `user.name` and `user.email` and run `git commit`; I wrote the message as \"fix(recover): suppress http.ErrAbortHandler in recover\". The repo is also on a detached HEAD, so create a branch first if you plan to open a PR.","ttft_ms":1985,"type":"result","duration_ms":35474,"uuid":"1bcacbaa-3c0d-4ddc-a867-3e0ea016b6ed","ttft_stream_ms":1381,"time_to_request_ms":144,"first_content_frame_ms":1382,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
